---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site-config';
import { SITES } from '../lib/sites';
import { breadcrumbJsonLd, organizationJsonLd, serviceJsonLd, faqPageJsonLd } from '../lib/seo';

const canonical = `${SITES.main}/export/`;

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Which Incoterm should a first charcoal order use?',
    a: 'For a containerised first order, FCA is usually the cleanest term to start from: the seller clears export and hands the container to the carrier, and the buyer controls the main carriage and insurance from a point they can see. FOB is still widely quoted for charcoal, but it was written for goods craned over a ship\'s rail, and for a container that is handed to a terminal days before sailing it can leave a gap where neither side is clearly responsible for the box. CIF is convenient but sets a minimum insurance cover that is narrower than most buyers assume. Whatever term is agreed, it belongs in the sales contract and in the quotation, not only in an email.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Who is the importer of record?',
    a: 'Normally the buyer, or the buyer\'s appointed entity in the destination country. It is the party that owes the duty, holds the import licence where one is required, and answers to customs for the declaration. A DDP quote moves much of the practical work to the seller, but it does not automatically make the seller the importer of record in every market, and a seller cannot lend its identity to a buyer who has no presence in the destination. Confirm who declares the goods before the container is booked, because the answer decides which documents are needed and whose name is on them.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is charcoal really dangerous goods?',
    a: 'Yes. Charcoal of animal or vegetable origin is UN 1361, Class 4.2 — substances liable to spontaneous combustion — packing group III. Since IMDG Amendment 42-24, the older route that allowed a product to ship outside the dangerous-goods rules on the strength of a self-heating test has been withdrawn, so every consignment is declared and carried as dangerous goods, and unpackaged bulk carriage in containers is no longer an option. The practical consequence is a declaration, a safety data sheet, marked packaging, a carrier that accepts Class 4.2, and a forwarder who books the cargo as what it is.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can the supplier ship without declaring it as dangerous goods?',
    a: 'A supplier who offers that is proposing a misdeclared booking, which is illegal and is the failure mode the rules exist to prevent. A misdeclared container can be refused at the terminal, held at transhipment, fined, or shut out of the vessel, and the cargo\'s self-heating risk does not disappear because the paperwork is wrong. It is also a straightforward competence signal: an exporter set up to handle charcoal will declare UN 1361 without being asked, and will have current self-heating documentation available when a carrier requests it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much charcoal fits in a container?',
    a: 'There is no single number, and any page that gives one without a packaging basis is guessing. Public industry guides report roughly 17–20 tonnes in a 20 ft container for coconut shell briquettes depending on whether the load is bulk bagged or retail boxed, and higher figures for a 40 ft high-cube; one public supplier planning figure used earlier in this network was 14.4 tonnes and 23.4 tonnes, which reflects a denser retail pack and a conservative loading method. The load plan for your order is calculated from the packed unit, the cartons or pallets per container, and the forwarder\'s weight and volume limits — then written down before booking.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = [
  breadcrumbJsonLd([
    { name: 'Home', url: `${SITES.main}/` },
    { name: 'Export', url: canonical },
  ]),
  organizationJsonLd({
    id: SITES.main,
    name: 'Charcoal Hub',
    description: site.description,
  }),
  serviceJsonLd({
    id: canonical,
    name: 'Charcoal Export & Shipping Support',
    serviceType: 'Charcoal export documentation and shipment planning',
    description: 'Export support for charcoal buyers — Incoterm and risk review, the export document set, UN 1361 Class 4.2 declaration, container loading plans and destination compliance checks.',
    providerId: SITES.main,
  }),
  faqPageJsonLd({ id: `${canonical}#faq`, faqs }),
];
---

<BaseLayout
  site={site}
  title="Charcoal Export & Shipping: Incoterms, Documents, UN 1361"
  description="How a charcoal export shipment is quoted, documented and loaded: Incoterms and risk transfer, the document set, UN 1361 Class 4.2 and destination checks."
  canonical={canonical}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', url: '/' }, { name: 'Export', url: '/export/' }]}
>
  <div class="page-title container">
    <h1>Export &amp; Shipping</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A charcoal container is not finished when production ends. It has to be declared as dangerous
      goods, loaded to a plan, documented for the destination and handed over on terms that decide who
      carries the risk at each step. This page covers that side of the order — and states plainly which
      numbers are industry reference rather than commitments for your shipment.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>A Shipment Has Five Parties, Not Two</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Most first shipments go wrong at a handover rather than at the factory. The product is fine,
          the container is fine, and then the booking is made by one party, the declaration filed by
          another and the documents issued in a name that does not match the buyer. Charcoal adds a
          dangerous-goods layer on top, so the split of responsibility has to be written down before
          the first booking, not discovered at the port.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Party</th><th>Controls</th><th>What fails when it is unclear</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Seller / exporter</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Production, packing, export clearance, the goods until handover</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Packing that does not match the load plan; export documents inconsistent with the invoice</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Freight forwarder</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Booking, dangerous-goods declaration, carrier selection, sailing schedule</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Cargo booked as general goods; a carrier that does not accept Class 4.2</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Carrier / shipping line</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Stowage, vessel acceptance, the bill of lading</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Container shut out at the terminal; a bill of lading that does not match the booking</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Customs broker at destination</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Import declaration, duty classification, release</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Wrong tariff heading; missing certificate; storage charges while the file is corrected</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Importer of record</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Duty, import licence where required, product compliance in the destination</td>
              <td class="spec-value">A DDP quote that never established who is allowed to import; a consignee who cannot clear</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The importer-of-record question is the one buyers skip most often. It is not a formality: it
          decides whose name is on the customs declaration, who owes the duty, and who answers for the
          product once it is inside the market. A quote that says DDP without naming a registered
          importer is an incomplete quote, however competitive the number looks.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Incoterms: Where the Risk Changes Hands</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          An Incoterm does three jobs at once: it says who arranges and pays for each leg, it names the
          point where risk passes from seller to buyer, and it fixes which documents the seller must
          provide. It does not set the price, the quality, the payment method or the law of the
          contract — those are separate clauses. The table below describes the 2020 rules in the way
          they behave for a container of charcoal, not as a recommendation for your order.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Term</th><th>Seller arranges</th><th>Buyer arranges</th><th>Risk passes</th><th>Charcoal-specific catch</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>EXW</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Making the goods available at its premises</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Everything, including export clearance</td>
              <td class="spec-value">At the seller's premises</td>
              <td class="spec-value">The buyer must be able to clear export in China; most overseas buyers cannot</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>FCA</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Export clearance, delivery to the carrier or terminal named</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Main carriage, insurance, import</td>
              <td class="spec-value">On handover to the carrier</td>
              <td class="spec-value">The ICC's preferred term for containerised cargo; needs a named delivery point</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>FOB</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Export clearance, delivery on board the vessel</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Main carriage, insurance, import</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Once goods are on board</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Written for break-bulk; a container is at the terminal days before loading, so the gap is real</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>CFR</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Export clearance, main carriage to destination port</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Insurance, import, onward carriage</td>
              <td class="spec-value">On board at origin</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Buyer carries sea risk without automatic cover; the cheapest freight route is not always the safest</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>CIF</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Export clearance, carriage, minimum marine insurance</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Import, onward carriage, any cover above the minimum</td>
              <td class="spec-value">On board at origin</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Minimum cover is 110% of contract value on Institute Cargo Clauses (C), which excludes many partial losses</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>DAP</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Carriage to the named destination, export clearance</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Import clearance, duty, unloading where agreed</td>
              <td class="spec-value">At the destination, ready for unloading</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Seller carries the whole transit risk; the buyer must still be able to import</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>DDP</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Carriage, import clearance and duty where the seller can act as importer</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Unloading, onward distribution</td>
              <td class="spec-value">At the destination, duty paid</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Requires a seller entity able to import in that market; local VAT and product rules still apply</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Two details decide whether the term actually protects anyone. The first is the named place:
          “FCA Shanghai” is not a term until it says which terminal or warehouse, because that is where
          the handover happens. The second is insurance. CIF's minimum is a floor, not a standard, and
          a container of charcoal that self-heats in transit can produce a claim that a narrow cargo
          clause does not meet. If the goods value is significant, read the cover before the term is
          agreed rather than after the loss.
        </p>
        <p>
          None of this is legal advice, and none of it fixes the term for your order. The applicable
          Incoterm, named place, insurance basis and payment method belong in the sales contract; the
          <a href="/request-quote/">quote request</a> is where we confirm which terms the order can be
          written on.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>The Document Set, and What Each Document Proves</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Charcoal paperwork is usually described as one list, but it is really three sets that do
          different jobs. Transport documents get the container onto the vessel. Origin and commercial
          documents get it through the border and release the payment. Market-compliance documents are
          what the destination authority asks for after that. Mixing them up produces the classic
          delay: a complete-looking file that is missing the one document the port actually wanted.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Document</th><th>Issued by</th><th>What it proves</th><th>What it does not prove</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Commercial invoice</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Seller</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Value, parties, terms of sale</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Origin, quality or compliance</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Packing list</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Seller</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Cartons, pallets, net and gross weight per line</td>
              <td class="spec-value">That the count is correct — that is what inspection checks</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Bill of lading</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Carrier or forwarder</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Receipt of the cargo, contract of carriage, title</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Condition of the goods inside a sealed container</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Certificate of origin</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Chamber of commerce or authorised body</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Country of origin for duty treatment</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Quality, safety or sustainability</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Dangerous goods declaration</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Shipper, via the forwarder</td>
              <td class="spec-value">That the cargo is declared as UN 1361, Class 4.2</td>
              <td class="spec-value">That the goods are dry, weathered or safe to load</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Safety data sheet (MSDS)</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Seller or manufacturer</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Composition, hazards, handling and stowage advice</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Batch-specific measurement</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Certificate of analysis</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Laboratory, or the seller if self-declared</td>
              <td class="spec-value">The tested parameters of the sampled lot, by a named method</td>
              <td class="spec-value">The whole lot, the factory, or the next order</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Fumigation / phytosanitary certificate</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Authorised treatment provider</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Treatment of wood packaging or a pest-risk requirement</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Anything about the charcoal itself</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Inspection report</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Third-party inspection body</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Count, condition, marks and photographs of the inspected lot</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Chemistry, unless samples were drawn and tested</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Insurance certificate</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Insurer or broker</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Cover, sum insured, clauses and exclusions</td>
              <td class="spec-value">That a particular loss is covered — read the clauses</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          One document deserves a separate warning. A certificate of analysis is not a certificate of
          the factory, the batch you bought, or the next shipment. It is a record of what a laboratory
          measured on a sample, under a method, on a date. The
          <a href="/charcoal-testing-coa-guide/">testing and COA guide</a> works through how to read one,
          and the <a href={`${SITES.testing}/tests/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">test method reference</a>
          gives the method behind each parameter. If the file has a COA but no sampling record, you know
          what was measured but not what it represents.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the destination side, the
          <a href={`${SITES.knowledge}/knowledge/charcoal-import-certifications/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">import certification guide</a>
          separates transport documents from market-compliance documents and lists what different
          markets typically ask for. The <a href="/compliance/">compliance notes</a> on this site cover
          the claims we will and will not make about them.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>UN 1361, Class 4.2: The Classification That Sets the Rules</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Charcoal of animal or vegetable origin — coconut shell hookah cubes included — is classified
          as <strong>UN 1361, “Carbon, animal or vegetable origin”, Class 4.2</strong>, substances liable
          to spontaneous combustion, packing group III. The hazard is not theoretical: fresh product,
          trapped moisture and poor ventilation in a sealed box can raise the internal temperature over
          a long voyage.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Field</th><th>Entry</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>UN number</td><td class="spec-value">1361</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Proper shipping name</td><td class="spec-value">Carbon, animal or vegetable origin</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Hazard class</td><td class="spec-value">4.2 — substances liable to spontaneous combustion</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Packing group</td><td class="spec-value">III</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Since IMDG Amendment 42-24, the old exemption path — test the product for self-heating and
          ship outside the dangerous-goods rules — has been withdrawn. Every consignment is declared and
          carried as dangerous goods regardless of any earlier test result, and unpackaged bulk
          carriage in a container is no longer an option. Shipments move under the process controls that
          replaced the exemption, and major carriers began enforcing the change through 2025.
        </p>
        <p>
          In practice that means four things on a charcoal order. The booking is made as Class 4.2, not
          as general cargo. Every bag or carton carries the UN 1361 marking and the Class 4.2 diamond
          alongside the trade label. The container carries the required placards. And the shipper can
          produce current self-heating documentation when the carrier asks for it. A supplier who
          offers to “ship it normally and save the DG fee” is offering a misdeclared booking, which can
          be refused, held, fined or shut out — and which leaves the fire risk exactly where it was.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is logistics context, not legal advice. Requirements vary by carrier and flag state, and
          the current IMDG text is the authority. The
          <a href={`${SITES.knowledge}/knowledge/charcoal-shipping-safety-un1361/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">UN 1361 shipping guide</a>
          sets out the classification and the 2025–2026 change in more detail; your forwarder confirms
          what applies to your route.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>The Loading Plan: Weight, Volume and Packaging</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Charcoal is a volume-constrained cargo. It cubes out before it weighs out, which means the
          number of tonnes in a container is decided by how densely the packed unit stacks, not by the
          container's payload rating. Retail boxes add air. Pallets consume usable volume — public
          estimates put the loss at 15–20% — and buy faster discharge and less damage at destination.
          Bulk bags pack densest and present worst on a retail shelf.
        </p>
        <p>
          Published figures differ for exactly that reason, and the difference is worth showing rather
          than hiding behind a single number.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Basis</th><th>20 ft</th><th>40 ft HQ</th><th>Status</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Coconut shell briquettes, full retail packaging</td>
              <td class="spec-value">~17–18.5 t</td>
              <td class="spec-value">~25–26 t</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Reported industry range, floor-stuffed</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Coconut shell briquettes, bulk packaging</td>
              <td class="spec-value">~19.5–20 t</td>
              <td class="spec-value">~26–28 t</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Reported industry range, floor-stuffed</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Earlier planning figure used in this network</td>
              <td class="spec-value">~14.4 t</td>
              <td class="spec-value">~23.4 t</td>
              <td class="spec-value">One public supplier figure, denser pack and conservative method</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The gap between 14.4 and 20 tonnes is not an error in either source; it is the packaging and
          loading method showing up in the arithmetic. That is why the load plan for an order is built
          from the packed unit — cartons per layer, layers per pallet or stack, pallets per container,
          gross weight per unit — and then checked against the forwarder's weight and volume limits.
          A load plan in tonnes without a unit count cannot be reconciled against the packing list, and
          the packing list is what the inspection checks.
        </p>
        <p>
          Four things belong in the plan before the container is stuffed. The dangerous-goods stowage
          requirement, because it can restrict how the cargo is distributed. The moisture and
          weathering evidence for the lot, because wet charcoal is both heavier and a greater
          self-heating risk. The seal number and the stuffing photographs, because they are the only
          record of what went in. And the discharge method at destination, because a floor-stuffed
          container that arrives where no one can unload it is a plan that failed at the last step.
        </p>
        <p>
          The arithmetic is worked through in the
          <a href={`${SITES.knowledge}/knowledge/charcoal-container-loading/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">container loading guide</a>,
          and the packing formats that change it are on the
          <a href="/packaging/">packaging page</a> and in the
          <a href={`${SITES.data}/packaging/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">packaging records</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Destination Checks Before You Book</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Every destination asks a different set of questions, and the answers are cheaper to find
          before the booking than after the arrival notice. Six checks cover most of the risk. A
          <a href="/request-quote/">quote request</a> that names the destination port, the packed
          format and the first volume is enough for us to say which of them apply before a price is
          discussed.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Check</th><th>Why it matters</th><th>Who confirms it</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Tariff classification</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Charcoal sits under heading 4402 in the HS schedule (HTS in the US, TARIC in the EU); the subheading drives duty and any trade measure</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Your customs broker</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Dangerous-goods acceptance</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Not every port, terminal or line accepts Class 4.2, and transhipment ports add their own rules</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Forwarder and carrier</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Import licence or permit</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Some markets require registration or a permit before charcoal can be imported at all</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Importer of record, broker</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Product labelling</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Retail packs need the destination's language, importer details and origin marking; the EU GPSR adds a responsible-person requirement</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Buyer, with the label proof</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Wood packaging</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Pallets and crates need ISPM 15 treatment and the mark, or the consignment can be refused</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Seller and treatment provider</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Deforestation and due diligence</td>
              <td class="spec-value">The EU regulation on deforestation-free products covers listed commodities; whether a coconut-shell charcoal consignment falls in scope is a determination for the importer</td>
              <td class="spec-value">Importer, with counsel</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Two of these deserve a longer note. Labelling is a production decision, not a shipping one: a
          retail carton printed before the destination's requirements were checked cannot be fixed at
          the port, and a reprint is a new print run. The
          <a href="/private-label/">private label</a> and <a href="/oem/">OEM</a> pages treat the label
          proof as a gate for that reason.
        </p>
        <p>
          The due-diligence question is the other one. Rules that attach to a commodity are assessed
          against the actual product and supply chain, not against a category name, and the party that
          files the due-diligence statement is the importer. We will supply the product and origin
          information the file needs; we will not tell a buyer that a consignment is “EUDR compliant”
          on the strength of a product name. That determination belongs with the importer and its
          counsel, and the
          <a href={`${SITES.knowledge}/knowledge/charcoal-import-certifications/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">import certification guide</a>
          explains where it sits among the other documents.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Documents Against Payment</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Where payment runs on a letter of credit, the document set becomes the payment mechanism, and
          a single mismatch can stop it. The classic discrepancies are mundane: a weight on the packing
          list that differs from the bill of lading, a description that does not match the credit word
          for word, a certificate issued after the shipment date, an insurance certificate for less
          than the credit requires. None of them is a quality problem, and all of them can leave a
          container sitting at destination while the banks exchange amendments.
        </p>
        <p>
          The defence is a pre-shipment document check against the credit terms, run before the
          container leaves, with the invoice, packing list, bill of lading draft, certificate of origin
          and any inspection or analysis document read side by side. If the credit calls for a
          certificate of analysis, agree the laboratory, the parameters and the sampling point before
          production — not after, when the only available report covers a different lot.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>What This Page Does Not Claim</h2>
      <div class="callout-pending">
        <span class="callout-title">Data pending — scope and evidence</span>
        <p>
          Charcoal Hub is a sourcing and product-intelligence platform, not a freight forwarder, a
          customs broker or a carrier. We do not publish a licence number, a carrier contract, a
          freight rate, a transit time for a named lane or an insurance facility, because none is
          verified in the records. Container tonnages, the 15–25 day production window after artwork
          approval and the pallet-volume loss are industry reference or planning figures, not
          commitments. The classification and regulatory points are logistics context compiled from
          public sources, not legal advice. Rates, routings, terms and the document set are confirmed
          per shipment with the forwarder, the broker and the carrier.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Export Questions</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <div>
            <h3>{f.q}</h3>
            <p>{f.a}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="cta-banner">
        <h2>Shipping to a new market?</h2>
        <p>
          Send the destination country or port, the packed format and the first volume. We will confirm
          which parts of the shipment can be planned now, which need a document from the supplier, and
          which are the importer's to determine.
        </p>
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/request-quote/">Request a Quote →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
